/** \addtogroup area_optimization */
/** \defgroup theory_optimization Theory of optimization
 \ingroup area_optimization

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/optimization` solve problems of
 optimization by the two techniques of chapters 13 and 14 of the book: the
 dynamic programming, which solves every subproblem once and combines the
 solutions of the smaller ones (the knapsack and its relatives, the weighted
 intervals, the optimal search tree, the partition of a sequence, the games
 and the problems on rows and grids), and the greedy algorithms, which fix
 one component of the solution at a time and never revise it (the
 fractional knapsack, the intervals, the order of the programs, the cover of
 a segment). This page collects what the headers take for granted: the
 notation shared by them (see \ref theory_optimization_notation), the
 principle on which every dynamic programming rests and the argument behind
 its costs (see \ref theory_optimization_dp and
 \ref theory_optimization_dp_families), and the exchange arguments that
 prove a greedy choice optimal, together with the cases where no greedy choice
 is (see \ref theory_optimization_greedy). The pseudocode of each algorithm
 is instead on the page of its header, in the comment of its function,
 together with the section of the book, or the paper, that it follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_optimization_notation Definitions and notation

 A problem of optimization asks, for an instance, a feasible solution of
 largest (or least) measure; its <em>subproblems</em> are the instances of the
 same problem on a part of the data (the first \f$ i \f$ objects, the keys from
 \f$ i \f$ to \f$ j \f$, the capacity \f$ c \f$), and a dynamic programming
 keeps the optimal value of each subproblem in a <em>table</em>, indexed by the
 parameters that name it.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ n \f$</td>
     <td>the number of the objects, the items, the intervals or the keys of
         the instance</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ p_i \f$, \f$ v_i \f$, \f$ C \f$</td>
     <td>the profit and the volume of the object \f$ i \f$, and the capacity
         of the knapsack</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathit{DP}[ i ][ c ] \f$, \f$ D[ \cdot ] \f$</td>
     <td>the table of a dynamic programming, the optimal value of the
         subproblem named by its indices</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ [ a_i , b_i ) \f$, \f$ w_i \f$</td>
     <td>an interval of the line, open on the right unless the header says
         otherwise, and its weight</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathit{pred}[ i ] \f$</td>
     <td>the last interval, in the order of the right ends, that ends before
         the interval \f$ i \f$ starts</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ t_i \f$, \f$ C_i \f$</td>
     <td>the duration of the program \f$ i \f$ and its time of
         completion</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ W( i , j ) \f$, \f$ R[ i ][ j ] \f$</td>
     <td>the weight of the keys and of the gaps from \f$ i \f$ to \f$ j \f$
         of a search tree, and a best root of those keys</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_optimization_dp Dynamic programming

 The dynamic programming applies when the problem has <em>optimal
 substructure</em>, as follows.

 <em>Lemma 1 (the principle of optimality).</em> If an optimal solution of an
 instance is made of a choice and of the solution of a subproblem that the
 choice leaves, then that solution is optimal for the subproblem.

 Indeed, a better solution of the subproblem, combined with the same choice,
 would give a better solution of the instance. Hence the optimal value of an
 instance is the best, over the choices, of the value of the choice combined
 with the optimal value of what it leaves, a recurrence; for the 0-1 knapsack
 (`knapsack()` of `Knapsack.h`, section 13.6), the choice is whether to take
 the last object, and
 \f[
   \mathit{DP}[ i ][ c ] = \max\{ \mathit{DP}[ i - 1 ][ c ] ,
   \mathit{DP}[ i - 1 ][ c - v_i ] + p_i \} , \qquad
   \mathit{DP}[ 0 ][ c ] = 0 , \tag{1}
 \f]
 with \f$ \mathit{DP}[ i - 1 ][ c - v_i ] = -\infty \f$ when
 \f$ v_i > c \f$. The subproblems of a recurrence such as (1) overlap, i.e.,
 the same one is reached by many paths of choices, so that the recursion
 written as it stands takes exponential time; the dynamic programming solves
 each of them once, either bottom up, filling the table in an order in which
 every entry comes after those it reads, or top down, by the recursion with
 a memo of the values already found (`zaino()`, the memoization of the book,
 which keeps the memo in a hash table and solves only the subproblems that
 the recursion reaches). The cost is the number of the subproblems times the
 cost of one recurrence, i.e., \f$ \Theta( n C ) \f$ for (1), and the
 solution itself is read back from the table by retracing the choices that
 gave each optimum. The subproblems and their dependences form a DAG, and
 a dynamic programming is a longest or a shortest path in it, which is how
 the problems of `HouseProblems.h` read.

 A bound such as \f$ \Theta( n C ) \f$ is <em>pseudopolynomial</em>: it is
 polynomial in the value \f$ C \f$, but \f$ C \f$ takes only \f$ \log C \f$
 bits of the input, and the bound is exponential in the size of the input.
 This is no defect of the algorithm, since the decision version of the 0-1
 knapsack is NP-complete; the counting versions (`coinChangeWays()`,
 `subsetSumCount()` of `KnapsackVariants.h`) share the same bound.

 \section theory_optimization_dp_families The families of recurrences

 The recurrences of the headers fall into a few families, which differ in the
 shape of the subproblems and therefore in the cost.

 <table>
 <caption>The dynamic programmings of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Subproblems</th><th>Functions</th><th>Table</th><th>Time</th></tr>
 <tr><td>a prefix and a capacity</td><td><tt>knapsack()</tt>,
     <tt>subsetSumCount()</tt></td><td>\f$ n \times C \f$, or one row</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( n C ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>a capacity, items in unlimited supply</td>
     <td><tt>unboundedKnapsack()</tt>, <tt>rodCutting()</tt>,
     <tt>coinChangeWays()</tt>, <tt>optimalChange()</tt></td>
     <td>one row of \f$ C + 1 \f$</td><td>\f$ \Theta( n C ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>a prefix of a sequence</td>
     <td><tt>maxNonAdjacentSum()</tt>, <tt>paintHouses()</tt>,
     <tt>maxinterval()</tt>, <tt>maxProfit()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ n \f$ (times the states)</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n ) \f$ to \f$ O( n k ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>a prefix, the best predecessor among all</td>
     <td><tt>largestDivisibleSubset()</tt>,
     <tt>longestChainSubsequence()</tt></td><td>\f$ n \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( n^2 ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>a range \f$ [ i , j ) \f$</td>
     <td><tt>coinsInALine()</tt>, <tt>optimalSearchTree()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ n \times n \f$</td><td>\f$ \Theta( n^2 ) \f$ to
     \f$ O( n^3 ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>a prefix and a number of parts</td>
     <td><tt>linearPartition()</tt></td><td>\f$ k \times n \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( k n^2 ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>a cell of a grid</td><td><tt>largestSquare()</tt>,
     <tt>countLatticePaths()</tt></td><td>one row of \f$ n \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( m n ) \f$</td></tr>
 </table>

 Two remarks explain the bounds that are better than the size of the table
 times the number of the choices. First, when the subproblems are the
 prefixes and the choices a set of predecessors, a structure on the
 predecessors may answer the best of them faster than the scan: in the
 weighted intervals of section 13.2 (`maxinterval()` of
 `WeightedIntervals.h`),
 \f[
   \mathit{DP}[ i ] = \max\{ \mathit{DP}[ i - 1 ] ,
   w_i + \mathit{DP}[ \mathit{pred}[ i ] ] \} , \tag{2}
 \f]
 the only compatible predecessor that counts is the last one, which one scan
 of the sorted ends gives for all the intervals at once; in
 `maxSumIncreasingSubsequence()` a Fenwick tree of the prefix maxima gives the
 best smaller value in \f$ O( \log n ) \f$; in `paintHouses()` the two least
 entries of the row before give the least over the colors but one in
 \f$ O( 1 ) \f$. Second, the order in which a table is filled decides what it
 counts: `coinChangeWays()` adds the coins one kind at a time, with the
 amounts going up, so that each multiset is counted once and in a single
 order, while `subsetSumCount()` runs the amounts down, so that each item
 is used at most once, which is the row of (1) kept in place.

 For the ranges, the optimal search tree of `OptimalSearchTree.h` has
 \f[
   C[ i ][ j ] = W( i , j ) + \min_{i \le r < j} \bigl( C[ i ][ r ]
   + C[ r + 1 ][ j ] \bigr) , \tag{3}
 \f]
 \f$ O( n ) \f$ choices for each of the \f$ O( n^2 ) \f$ ranges, i.e.,
 \f$ O( n^3 ) \f$; the bound falls by the following property.

 <em>Theorem 2 (Knuth, Yao).</em> If \f$ W \f$ is monotone on the inclusion
 of the ranges and satisfies the quadrangle inequality
 \f$ W( i , j ) + W( i' , j' ) \le W( i' , j ) + W( i , j' ) \f$ for
 \f$ i \le i' \le j \le j' \f$, then the largest best root of (3) satisfies
 \f[
   R[ i ][ j - 1 ] \le R[ i ][ j ] \le R[ i + 1 ][ j ] . \tag{4}
 \f]

 Hence the roots of the ranges of one length \f$ h \f$ are looked for in
 consecutive intervals of candidates, whose lengths
 \f$ R[ i + 1 ][ i + h ] - R[ i ][ i + h - 1 ] + 1 \f$ telescope to
 \f$ O( n ) \f$ over \f$ i \f$; the \f$ n \f$ lengths cost \f$ O( n^2 ) \f$
 in all. The weights of the keys and of the gaps satisfy the hypothesis with
 equality, and the partition of `linearPartition()` would admit the same
 speedup for items not negative, as its header remarks.

 \section theory_optimization_greedy Greedy algorithms

 A greedy algorithm builds a solution by stages and fixes at each one the
 component that looks best, with no going back; it is correct when the
 following property holds.

 <em>Lemma 3 (the greedy choice).</em> If for every instance some optimal
 solution contains the greedy choice, and the rest of it is an optimal
 solution of the subproblem that the choice leaves, then the greedy algorithm
 is optimal.

 Indeed, by induction on the stages, the solution built so far is contained
 in some optimal solution. The first hypothesis is proved by an
 <em>exchange argument</em>: an optimal solution without the greedy choice is
 changed, by swapping one of its components for the greedy one, into another
 solution that is feasible and no worse. The headers of the area rest on the
 following exchanges.

 - The intervals (`independentSet()` of `Intervals.h`, section 14.2): the
   interval that ends first can replace the first interval of any
   independent set, since it ends no later and the others start after that
   one ends; hence the greedy by increasing right end is optimal, in
   \f$ O( n \log n ) \f$ for the sort. The cover of a segment
   (`minimumIntervalCover()` of `IntervalCover.h`) is its dual: the interval
   that starts within the covered part and ends farthest can replace the
   interval of any cover that contains the first uncovered point.
 - The fractional knapsack (`fractionalKnapsack()`, section 14.1): if an
   optimal solution has \f$ x_j > 0 \f$ while an object \f$ i \f$ of larger
   \f$ p_i / v_i \f$ is not whole, moving a volume
   \f$ \varepsilon \f$ from \f$ j \f$ to \f$ i \f$ changes the profit by
   \f$ \varepsilon ( p_i / v_i - p_j / v_j ) \ge 0 \f$; hence the objects by
   decreasing specific profit, the last one in part.
 - The order of the programs (`ShortestJobFirst.h`, exercise 14.5): swapping
   two adjacent programs \f$ a \f$ and \f$ b \f$ leaves the times of
   completion of the others unchanged, and changes the weighted sum by
   \f[
     w_a t_b - w_b t_a , \tag{5}
   \f]
   which is not negative exactly when \f$ t_a / w_a \le t_b / w_b \f$;
   hence an order with an adjacent pair against Smith's rule is improved by
   the swap, and the order by increasing \f$ t_i / w_i \f$ is optimal (by
   increasing \f$ t_i \f$ for unit weights).
 - The coloring of the intervals (`intervalColoring()`, exercise 14.6): the
   greedy reuses a free color whenever one exists, and opens a new one only
   when all the colors in use are held by intervals over the current point;
   hence it uses as many colors as the largest number of intervals over a
   point, a lower bound for any coloring.

 The greedy choice is not optimal for every problem, and the headers show
 where it fails. For the change with the fewest coins (`CoinChange.h`) the
 largest coin first is optimal for the coins of 50, 10, 5 and 1, but pays 15
 with five coins of 11, 1, 1, 1, 1 instead of three of 5, where the dynamic
 programming of `optimalChange()` is needed; for the 0-1 knapsack no order of
 the objects is optimal (exercise 14.1); and the sum of four squares of
 `minimumSquares()` is not found by taking the largest square first,
 \f$ 18 = 9 + 9 \f$ rather than \f$ 16 + 1 + 1 \f$. The set cover, of which
 the cover of a segment is the case on the line, is NP-hard in general, and
 the greedy that takes the set with the most new elements approximates it
 within a factor \f$ \ln n \f$ (Chvátal).

 <table>
 <caption>The greedy algorithms of the area and their arguments</caption>
 <tr><th>Function</th><th>Greedy order</th><th>Argument</th>
     <th>Time</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>fractionalKnapsack()</tt></td><td>decreasing
     \f$ p_i / v_i \f$</td><td>exchange of volume</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n \log n ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>independentSet()</tt></td><td>increasing right end</td>
     <td>exchange of the first interval</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n \log n ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>minimumIntervalCover()</tt></td><td>the farthest end among
     those that start in time</td><td>exchange of the interval over the
     first uncovered point</td><td>\f$ O( n \log n ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>intervalColoring()</tt></td><td>the ends by position</td>
     <td>the depth is a lower bound</td><td>\f$ O( n \log n ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>towers()</tt></td><td>increasing position</td>
     <td>exchange of the first tower</td><td>\f$ O( n \log n ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>shortestJobFirst()</tt>, <tt>smithOrder()</tt></td>
     <td>increasing \f$ t_i / w_i \f$</td><td>exchange of adjacent
     programs, (5)</td><td>\f$ O( n \log n ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>greedyChange()</tt></td><td>decreasing coin</td>
     <td>optimal for some systems of coins only</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n \log n + c ) \f$</td></tr>
 </table>

